Case Scenario Analysis
Case 1: Gastrointestinal Symptoms Associated with Ibuprofen
Patient details: A 45-year-old male was taking treatment for knee pain.
Medication history: The patient was prescribed ibuprofen 400 mg orally three times daily for five days.
Presenting symptoms: After taking ibuprofen for approximately three days, the patient developed stomach pain, nausea, and a burning sensation in the upper abdomen.
Assessment of the suspected ADR:
Ibuprofen belongs to the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) group. Although it is commonly used to relieve pain and inflammation, it can cause gastrointestinal problems by reducing the production of prostaglandins that help protect the stomach lining.
In this case, the symptoms appeared after the patient started taking ibuprofen. The timing and nature of the symptoms suggest that the medicine may have contributed to the gastrointestinal discomfort. However, other conditions, such as gastritis or peptic ulcer disease, could also produce similar symptoms.
Information about whether the symptoms improved after stopping ibuprofen was not available. Therefore, the relationship between ibuprofen and the reaction cannot be confirmed.
Assessment parameter	Findings
Suspected medicine	Ibuprofen
Suspected reaction	Gastrointestinal irritation or dyspepsia
Time of onset	Approximately three days after starting treatment
Possible relationship	Compatible with a drug-related reaction
Other possible causes	Gastritis, peptic ulcer disease, or other medicines
Provisional causality	Possible
Conclusion for the case: Ibuprofen was identified as the suspected medicine because the patient developed gastrointestinal symptoms after starting treatment. Further clinical assessment would be required to determine whether the medicine was responsible.
 

